    "body": "The latest version of the Department of Labor’s (DOL) independent contractor rule was handed down Thursday with minuscule changes from what was proposed in February.\nThe shift from the Biden administration’s IC rule to that of the second Trump administration is generally viewed as giving the Wage & Hour division of the DOL, in its rulings, guidelines that are more favorable to a finding a worker is an independent contractor rather than an employee.\nThe IC rule for the Trump 47 administration is not seen as that much different from the rule it implemented in the Trump 45 administration. But that rule didn’t go into effect until Donald Trump was about to leave office.\nHowever, after significant back-and-forth in the court system, it was revived by a court order and was the DOL standard for more than two years before the Biden rule came into effect. That Biden rule is what will be replaced by the Trump 47 rule.\nATA at the ceremony\nThe final rule is considered positive enough for the trucking industry that the American Trucking Associations ’ new president and CEO Mark Colson took part in what the ATA said was a “celebration” in Washington at the DOL coinciding with the rule’s release.\n“A final independent contractor rule issued by the agency will rescind a destructive, Biden-era classification standard,” the ATA said in a prepared statement regarding Thursday’s action.\nAccording to the ATA, both Colson and ATA vice chairman Gregg Troian spoke at the Department of Labor “celebration.”\nWhile there was virtually no criticism coming from the trucking industry about the changes from the Biden rule, which were effectively baked in with the February notice of proposed rulemaking in the Federal Register, a consistent voice in the process has been that of attorney Richard Reibstein, a partner with the firm of Littler Mendelson and an expert on independent contractor status.\nHow much does it matter?\nThroughout the shifts between the Trump and Biden administrations, Reibstein has repeatedly delivered the same message: it’s not that big a deal.\nWhatever the DOL came up with “is unlikely to be given deference by the courts, which have historically chosen to apply their own judicial precedent rather than give deference to administrative rules on IC status that change back and forth when a new administration enters the White House,” Reibstein wrote on his blog soon after the IC rule was released Thursday. “Further, most IC misclassification lawsuits as well as agency investigations and proceedings are not brought only under the Fair Labor Standards Act but rather are commenced in whole or in part under state laws, most of which use different tests for IC status.”\nReibstein calculated that the final rule released this week had changed only three sentences and a few additional examples from the proposal of earlier this year.\nToday, I’m proud to announce @USDOL has FINALIZED our new INDEPENDENT CONTRACTOR RULE. This Administration is boldly taking the final step toward ENDING UNCERTAINTY by establishing CLEAR and EASY-TO-UNDERSTAND criteria for determining who is an EMPLOYEE and who is an INDEPENDENT… pic.twitter.com/0hxdQeGXqt — Secretary Keith Sonderling (@Sonderling47) November 19, 2026\nThe final rule will take place 60 days after it is published in the Federal Register.\nThe trucking industry came in for a notable mention in the almost 400 pages of the rule and its summary, with the DOL citing a lease-purchase agreement as not being a sign of control by the carrier that signs on a driver to such a contract. There had been comments submitted expressing concern over that possibility.\nLease purchase deals are OK\nAfter recapping some of the comments and queries made from trucking interests during the comment period, the DOL said it “generally believes that the fact that a driver owns a truck and exercises initiative in how to operate it seems to plainly indicate independent contractor status under the opportunity for profit or loss factor.”\nThe DOL said it would specifically add examples in the final rule that deal with a driver operating on a lease purchase agreement that “actually provide an opportunity for the worker to own the vehicle in the future.”\n“These facts would indicate independent contractor status under the opportunity for profit or loss,” the DOL said, expecting that there will now be “sufficient clarification in response to a multitude of comments on this topic.”\nWhen the proposed rule was announced in February, a key provision in contrast to the Biden rule was the emphasis on two factors of the “multi-factor economic realities test.”\nThat wasn’t new; it was part of the 2021 Trump 45 rule that was in effect until 2024.\nIn reviewing the view of the multi-factor economic realities test, the DOL said the 2021 rule “stated that if these two core factors point towards the same status (i.e., employee or independent contractor), there is a substantial likelihood that the indicated outcome is the worker’s accurate status.\nThe other three parts of the test that the DOL said are “less probative” and “non-core” are”the amount of skill required for the work, the degree of permanence of the working relationship between the worker and the potential employer, and whether the work is part of an integrated unit of production.”\nThose two factors, according to the Scopelitis Law Firm in an email about the rule and its impact on trucking, are “core guideposts which, if aligned in favor of independent contractor status or employee status, will frequently end the analysis.”\nTwo are more important than the others\nBut the Biden administration’s rule did not elevate any of the factors above all. That was viewed as making it more likely that the Wage & Hour division or other government agencies relying on the DOL rule would find a worker is an employee rather than an independent contractor.\nScopelitis also noted–as it and others had in February with the proposed rule–that the latest version of the DOL IC rule provided “certainty” on the question of control by “noting that compliance with specific legal obligations and health and safety standards (even voluntary standards) is not control evidencing an employee relationship.”\nThat had been a key concern under the Biden administration rule whether the employer of an independent owner operator mandating that the driver keep compliant with various regulations was a sign of control, which could undercut any argument that an employee was truly independent.\nIn a prepared statement, the Owner Operator Independent Drivers Association (OOIDA) expressed mostly support for the final rule.\nOOIDA particularly singled out the DOL clarifying that there is no federal rule requiring speed limiters. “DOL’s continued acknowledgement that speed limiters are not a mandated safety device is welcome,” OOIDA said, referring to a footnote that references to a speed limiter in the 2021 rule could have created “potential confusion over whether federal motor carrier safety regulations require the installation of speed limiting devices.”\nOOIDA’s endorsement of the rule was not at the level of ebullience of the ATA.\n“While the rule does not address all of our concerns, it also does not create new barriers for an owner-operator to work as an independent contractor,” OOIDA said.\nMore articles by John Kingston\nSCOTUS action revives shipper/receiver liability post-Montgomery\nATA spells out short- and long-term solutions to halt ELD fraud\nDenial of funds to NY over non domiciled CDLs overturned: appellate court",
